including the zetaregularized products, Kronecker’s limit formula, and the Selberg trace formula. The presentation of Iwasawa theory focuses on the Iwasawa main conjecture, which establishes far-reaching relations between a p-adic analytic zeta function and a determinant defined from a Galois action on some ideal class groups. This book also contains a short exposition on the arithmetic of elliptic curves and the proof of Fermat's last theorem by Wiles.\n\nTogether with the first two volumes, this book is a good resource for anyone learning or teaching modern algebraic number theory.\n\n## The Author(s)\n\nNobushige Kurokawa, Tokyo Institute of Technology, Japan\n\nMasato Kurihara, Keio University, Yokohama, Japan,\n\nTakeshi Saito, University of Tokyo, Japan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nlist of English publications (https://www.ms.u-tokyo.ac.jp/~t-saito/publ.html)\nciteturn642search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Kurokawa, translated by M. ... Number theory 3, Iwasawa Theory and Modular Forms\n  4. Wild ramification of schemes and sheaves, Proceedings of the international congress of mathematicians (ICM 2010), Hyderabad, 335-356\n  5. An introduction to Galois representations and modular forms. Autour des Motifs III, Panoramas et Syntheses 49, SMF 1-27.\n  6. Galois representations in arithmetic geometry. suams.pdf (published in Sugaku exposition) (01/04/26)\n  7. Galois representations in arithmetic geometry II (translated by Masato Kuwata). GR2.pdf (published in Sugaku exposition) (02/04/08)\n\n### Books\n\n  1. Ramification Groups of Local Fields with Geometric Applications, Cambridge University Press, New Mathematical Monographs 54 (2026) errata\n  2. Fermat's Last Theorem : Basic Tools (translated by M. Kuwata) (2013)\n  3. Fermat's Last Theorem : The proof (translated by M. Kuwata) (2014)\n  4. Number theory 1, Fermat's dream\n(with K. Kato, N. Kurokawa, translated by M. Kuwata) (2000)\n  5. Numbe