 Kurokawa and Takeshi Saito, Translations of  Mathematical Monographs 242 (2012)\n\nMasato Kurihara Website\n\n# Books\n\nBooks\n\n1. Development of Iwasawa Theory — the Centennial of K. Iwasawa's Birth\n\nAdvanced Studies in Pure Mathematics 86  [published on December 10, 2020] \n\n(This is the Proceedings of the conference Iwasawa 2017 held at the University of Tokyo in 2017.)\n\n2. Exploration of the Arithmetic World of Gauss (in Japanese; Sugaku Shobo, amazon) [published on May 15, 2017]\n\n(A n introduction to number theory for undergraduate students. B eg inning with a famous problem of d rawing regular polygons, this book gives elementary proo fs of both quadratic and biquadratic reciprocity laws based on Gauss's original idea, and a proof of the formula on the number of rational points over finite fields on some quartic curves, etc. (these are all related).)\n\n3. Hecke's L-functions, Spring 1964 by Kenkichi Iwasawa, edited with John Coates, (2019) \n\n(Lecture notes by K. Iwasawa himself in 1964 at Princeton University on the adelic approach to Hecke's L-functions, now called Iwasawa-Tate's method.) \n\n4. Number Theory 3: Iwasawa Theory and Modular Forms, \n\nwith Nobushige Kurokawa and Takeshi Saito, Translations of  Mathematical Monographs 242 (2012)\n\n5. Kenkichi Iwasawa Collected papers Vol. I, II, edited with Ichiro Satake, \n\nGenjiro Fujisaki, Kazuya Kato, and Shoichi Nakajima, (2001)\n\n6. Invitation to higher local fields, edited with Ivan Fesenko, (2000)  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniversities Press focusses on the publication of books in Science, Technology and (https://www.orientblackswan.com/downloads/Catalogues2024-25/Mathematics_2025.pdf)\nciteturn642search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Number Theory 3Nobushige Kurokawa ... this book are Iwasawa theory and modular forms.\nwww.universitiespress.com\n119  | \n--- | ---\nMathematics  | Number Theory in the Spirit of    \nRamanujan\nof Mathematical Monographs (Iwanami Series \nin Modern Mathematics), Vol. 186, American \nMathematical Society, 2000.\nBruce C Berndt\nUniversity of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, USA\n2017\n256 pp\nPaperback\n978-1-4704-3736-7 \n \n` 1,700.00\nNumber Theory 3        \nNobushige Kurokawa\nTokyo Institute of Technology, Tokyo, Japan\nMasato Kurihara\nKeio University, Yokohama, Japan \nTakeshi Saito\nUniversity of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan\nRamanujan is recognized as one of the great \nnumber theorists of the twentieth century. Here \nnow is the ﬁrst book to provide an introduction to \nhis work in number theory. Most of Ramanujan’s \nwork in number theory arose out of q-series \nand theta functions. Tis book provides an \nintroduction to these two important subjects and \nto some of the topics in number theory that are \ninextricably intertwined with them, including the \ntheory of partitions, sums of squares and triangular \nnumbers, and the Ramanujan tau function. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTopics in p-adic analysis (https://jscholarship.library.jhu.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/b19aaba7-7b42-4301-a0fd-838af87fd6c1/content)\nciteturn642search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.6 years ago; Nobushige Kurokawa, Masato Kurihara, and Takeshi Saito.Number theory. 3, volume 242 of Trans- ... In Development of Iwasawa Theory – the Centennial of K.\n"
